%section#how.band
  .prose
    %h2 How the tabs work
    %p
      You give the inside dimensions of the box, the empty space it has to hold, and the thickness of the material.
      Makeabox draws six panels whose edges are cut into tabs, also called notches or teeth, so each side snaps into its neighbours.
    %p
      Every edge is divided into an odd number of equal sections. That keeps each panel symmetric, which is what makes these
      boxes easy to put together: there are only ever three different panels, and none of them can go in backwards.
    %h3 Thickness
    %p
      Measure your sheet with calipers instead of trusting the label. Plywood sold as 1/4&Prime; is often closer to 0.2&Prime;,
      and the tabs are exactly as deep as the number you enter.
    %h3 Notch length
    %p
      Left blank, tabs are about three times the thickness. A value you enter is a guide: the width is adjusted so that
      a whole, odd number of tabs fits each edge. It can be anything from 10 mm (0.4 in) up to a third of the shortest side.
    %h3 Kerf
    %p
      The laser burns away a thin line of material. Kerf is the width of that line, and the drawing is widened by it so
      the tabs fit tightly. Cut a small test box first, then raise the kerf if the fit is loose.
    %p
      The drawing opens in LightBurn, Inkscape, Illustrator or CorelDRAW, where you can add holes and engraving before
      sending it to the cutter.
      = succeed '.' do
        %a{href: 'https://github.com/kigster/laser-cutter'} The code that draws it is open source
